White Noise in Time Series

White Noise is a completely random series of data points with:

✅ No trend
✅ No seasonality
✅ No pattern
✅ No relationship between past and future values

Because there is no pattern, it cannot be forecasted.

Simple Example

Imagine tossing a coin:

| Toss | Result |
| ---- | ------ |
| 1    | Head   |
| 2    | Tail   |
| 3    | Head   |
| 4    | Tail   |
| 5    | Head   |


There is no predictable pattern.

This is similar to White Noise.

=========

Real-Time Example

Suppose a shop's daily sales are:

| Day | Sales |
| --- | ----: |
| 1   |   102 |
| 2   |    97 |
| 3   |   105 |
| 4   |    99 |
| 5   |   101 |
| 6   |    96 |
| 7   |   104 |


The values randomly move up and down around the same average.

There is no clear trend or seasonality.

This resembles White Noise.

==============

Characteristics of White Noise

| Property           | White Noise |
| ------------------ | ----------- |
| Trend              | ❌ No        |
| Seasonality        | ❌ No        |
| Pattern            | ❌ No        |
| Mean               | Constant    |
| Variance           | Constant    |
| Forecast Possible? | ❌ No        |
| Stationary?        | ✅ Yes       |


=========

Why Is It Called "White" Noise?

Just as white light contains all colors mixed together,

white noise contains random frequencies mixed together without any recognizable pattern.

Examples:

TV static noise
Radio hiss
Fan background sound
